  "initiated_overall",     "Initiated Overall",                        "This plot shows the percent of people who have initiated LAI ART after being prescribed.",
  "sex8",                  "Initiated by Sex",                         "This plot shows the percent of people who have initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by sex. \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART  differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the sex variable, users can compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., female, male). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of sex).",
  "race8",                 "Initiated by Race",                        "This plot shows the percent of people who have initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by race. \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the race variable, users can compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., Black, White). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of race).",
  "ethnicity8",            "Initiated by Ethnicity",                   "This plot shows the percent of people who have been initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by ethnicity. \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the ethnicity variable, users can compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., Hispanic, Not Hispanic). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who ever initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of ethnicity).",
  "age8",                  "Initiated by Age",                         "This plot shows the percent of people who have been initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by age. \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the age variable, users can compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., age 18-24, 25-34). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of age).",
  "insurance8",            "Initiated by HIV Medication Payor",        "This plot shows the percent of people who have been initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by HIV medication payor. \n\nThe HIV medicator payor variable is created by combining two variables: health_care_coverage (the primary source of health care coverage the client had for any part of the reporting period; includes private insurance, Medicare, Medicaid, Veterans Health Administration, Indian Health Service, other health plans, or no insurance/uninsured) and secondary_payor (the payor of HIV medications if different or supplemental from health care coverage; includes ADAP and patient assistance programs). \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the HIV medicator payor variable, users can compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., private insurance, uninsured-ADAP). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of HIV medication payor).",
  "keypop8",               "Initiated by Key Population",              "This plot shows the percent of people who have been initiated LAI ART after being prescribed, by key population. \n\nKey populations include patients with different housing or employment status, poverty level, primary language, incarceration history, or other social determinants of health measured by the clinic. \n\nTo compare whether clients who have ever initiated LAI ART differ from clients who never initiated LAI ART for the key population variable, users can first select a key population (e.g., patients with different housing status) and then compare the bars (initiated LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., patients with different housing status). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients who initiated LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients ever initiated LAI ART regardless of key population).",
  "time8",                 "Initiated Over Time",                      "Initiated over time shows the number of people initiating by month, based on the date they received their first injection.",
  
  # ========== Sustained Section ==========
  "sustained_overall",     "Sustained Overall",                        "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated.",
  "sex9",                  "Sustained by Sex",                         "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by sex. \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the sex variable, users can compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., female, male). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of sex).",
  "race9",                 "Sustained by Race",                        "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by race. \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the race variable, users can compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., Black, White). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of race).",
  "ethnicity9",            "Sustained by Ethnicity",                   "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by ethnicity. \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the ethnicity variable, users can compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., Hispanic, Not Hispanic). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of ethnicity).",
  "age9",                  "Sustained by Age",                         "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by age. \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the age variable, users can compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., age 18-24, 25-34). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of age).",
  "insurance9",            "Sustained by HIV Medication Payor",        "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by HIV medication payor. \n\nThe HIV medicator payor variable is created by combining two variables: health_care_coverage (the primary source of health care coverage the client had for any part of the reporting period; includes private insurance, Medicare, Medicaid, Veterans Health Administration, Indian Health Service, other health plans, or no insurance/uninsured) and secondary_payor (the payor of HIV medications if different or supplemental from health care coverage; includes ADAP and patient assistance programs). \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the HIV medicator payor variable, users can compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., private insurance, uninsured-ADAP). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of HIV medication payor).",
  "keypop9",               "Sustained by Key Population",              "This plot shows the percent of people sustained on LAI ART after being initiated, by key population. \n\nKey populations include patients with different housing or employment status, poverty level, primary language, incarceration history, or other social determinants of health measured by the clinic. \n\nTo compare whether clients sustained on LAI ART differ from clients who discontinued LAI ART for the key population variable, users can first select a key population (e.g., patients with different housing status) and then compare the bars (sustained on LAI ART) for each variable category (e.g., patients with different housing status). Differences in these values represent a care gap where one variable category are over or under represented among clients sustained on LAI ART, relative to their representativeness at the clinic. The dotted line indicates the clinic average (patients sustained on LAI ART regardless of key population).",
  "time9",                 "Time Spent on iCAB/RPV",                   "Time spent on iCAB/RPV shows the number of days that each person has spent on iCAB/RPV in the data, to date. The horizontal axis shows the amount of time spent on iCAB/RPV. The color shows whether they are sustained or have discontinued. The heigh of the bar shows the number of people. This provides a high-level overview of how long clients at your clinic have been on iCAB/RPV.",
  "discontinued_reason",   "Reasons Discontinued",                     "Discontinued reasons plot displays the number and percent reporting each reason among those who discontinued LAI ART. \n\nNote that interpretation of this chart will depend on how the variable \"discontinued_reason\" was coded. A clinic could choose to collect the primary reason for discontinuation or multiple reasons for discontinuation. If a clinic collected data on multiple reasons for discontinuation, the interpretation of the plot is as follows: \n\u2022 If the primary reason is reported in the \"reason\" column, and other reasons are reported in the \"other\" column, then the denominator of the bar chart is the number of clients who discontinued iCAB/RPV. \n\u2022 If all discontinued reasons are reported, separated by commas, then the denominator of the bar chart is the total number of reasons provided for discontinuation among clients who discontinued iCAB/RPV.",
  
  # ========== Clinical Outcomes Section ==========
  "ontime_status",         "On Time Injections",                                 NA,
  "ontime_monthly",        "On Time by Days Since Prior Injection (Monthly)",    "\"On time injections by days since prior injection\" shows how early or late particular injections were, with the on time injection region shaded in gray. There is a plot for monthly injection intervals and a plot for bimonthly injection intervals. If a clinic observes many injections delivered late or injection delivered close to the end of the injection interval, they may want to consider clinic policies, for example scheduling clients at the beginning of the injection interval to provide more time for re-scheduling before the interval ends.",
  "ontime_bimonthly",      "On Time by Days Since Prior Injection (Bimonthly)",  "\"On time injections by days since prior injection\" shows how early or late particular injections were, with the on time injection region shaded in gray. There is a plot for monthly injection intervals and a plot for bimonthly injection intervals. If a clinic observes many injections delivered late or injection delivered close to the end of the injection interval, they may want to consider clinic policies, for example scheduling clients at the beginning of the injection interval to provide more time for re-scheduling before the interval ends. ",
  "late_pt",               "Late Injections by Client",                          "\"Late injections by client\" shows the distribution of late injections, by client. This plot allows clinics to examine whether late injections are concentrated among a few clients or whether many clients are experiencing late injections once or twice.",
  "early_pt",              "Early Injections by Client",                         "\"Early injections by client\" shows the distribution of early injections, by client. This plot allows clinics to examine whether early injections are concentrated among a few clients or whether many clients are experiencing early injections once or twice.",
  
  # ========== Viral Load Section ==========
  "vl_time_to_vs",         "Time to Viral Suppression",                          "This plot shows the percent of clients who achieve viral suppression at key time points, among clients who started iCAB/RPV with viremia. The number in the parentheses at the bottom of each bar indicates how many clients contributed to that data point.  The sooner the plot shows dark orange bars, the shorter the time to viral suppression for clients who initiate with viremia.  Ideally the time to viral suppression will be short and reach near 100%. \n\nThe plot is calculated using the Kaplan-Meier estimator. This gives a conservative, statistical estimate of the time to viral suppression, that accounts for the uncertainty and potential bias due to clients discontinuing before they reach suppression and clients having variable follow-up time. Therefore, the percentages in the plots are statistical estimates, not actual raw percentages and numbers. The number of people contributing data (in parentheses) can give a sense of how certain or uncertain the estimate is.",
  "time_to_el_vl1",        "Time to Elevated VL, initiated suppressed",          "This plot shows the percent of clients who had an elevated viral load at key time points, among clients who started iCAB/RPV virally suppressed. The number in the parentheses at the bottom of each bar indicates how many clients contributed to that data point.  The sooner the plot shows dark blue bars, the shorter the time to elevated viral load for clients who initiate while virally suppressed. Ideally time to first elevated viral load will be long, and the percentage will remain low. \n\nThe plot is calculated using the Kaplan-Meier estimator. This gives a conservative, statistical estimate of the time to first raised viral load, that accounts for the uncertainty and potential bias due to clients discontinuing before they have a raised viral load and clients having variable follow-up time. Therefore, the percentages in the plots are statistical estimates, not actual raw percentages and numbers. The number of people contributing data (in parentheses) can give a sense of how certain or uncertain the estimate is.",
  "time_to_el_vl2",        "Time to Elevated VL, initiated with viremia",        "This plot shows the percent of clients who had an elevated viral load at key time points, among clients who started iCAB/RPV with viremia. The number in the parentheses at the bottom of each bar indicates how many clients contributed to that data point.  The sooner the plot shows dark orange bars, the shorter the time to elevated viral load for clients who initiate with viremia. Ideally time to first elevated viral load will be long, and the percentage will remain low. \n\nThe plot is calculated using the Kaplan-Meier estimator. This gives a conservative, statistical estimate of the time to first raised viral load, that accounts for the uncertainty and potential bias due to clients discontinuing before they have a raised viral load and clients having variable follow-up time. Therefore, the percentages in the plots are statistical estimates, not actual raw percentages and numbers. The number of people contributing data (in parentheses) can give a sense of how certain or uncertain the estimate is.",
  "time_to_failure1",      "Time to Treatment Failure, initiated suppressed",    "This plot shows the percent of clients who had treatment failure at key time points, among clients who started iCAB/RPV virally suppressed. The number in the parentheses at the bottom of each bar indicates how many clients contributed to that data point.  The sooner the plot shows dark blue bars, the shorter the time to treatment failure for clients who initiate while virally suppressed. Ideally time to first treatment failure will be long, and the percentage will remain low. \n\nThe plot is calculated using the Kaplan-Meier estimator. This gives a conservative, statistical estimate of the time to first treatment failure, that accounts for the uncertainty and potential bias due to clients discontinuing before they have treatment failure and clients having variable follow-up time. Therefore, the percentages in the plots are statistical estimates, not actual raw percentages and numbers. The number of people contributing data (in parentheses) can give a sense of how certain or uncertain the estimate is.",
  "time_to_failure2",      "Time to Treatment Failure, initiated with viremia",  "This plot shows the percent of clients who had treatment failure at key time points, among clients who started iCAB/RPV with viremia. The number in the parentheses at the bottom of each bar indicates how many clients contributed to that data point.  The sooner the plot shows dark blue bars, the shorter the time to treatment failure for clients who initiate with viremia. Ideally time to first treatment failure will be long, and the percentage will remain low. \n\nThe plot is calculated using the Kaplan-Meier estimator. This gives a conservative, statistical estimate of the time to first treatment failure, that accounts for the uncertainty and potential bias due to clients discontinuing before they have treatment failure and clients having variable follow-up time. Therefore, the percentages in the plots are statistical estimates, not actual raw percentages and numbers. The number of people contributing data (in parentheses) can give a sense of how certain or uncertain the estimate is.",
  "clinic_level_vl",       "Clinic Level Viral Load",                            "This plot shows the count or percent of people with a viral load above/below a chosen threshold in a chosen time period (every 3 months, every 6 months, or every 12 months), stratified by whether or not they were ever on LAI ART during that time period. Three bar charts are shown: clients on LAI ART (left), clients on oral ART (middle), and all clients on ART (right). \n\nNote that, while each person can only contribute 1 most recent viral load result per time period, that does not mean that each person appears in every period. In fact, the population of people in each period can vary widely depending on who received a viral load test during that period, contributing to changes in percent or count suppressed. Therefore, this metric should be considered preliminary and interpreted with caution."
) |>
  mutate(
    page = case_when(
      str_detect(section_id, "lai_care_gap|assessed_outcomes") ~ "lai_overview",
      str_detect(section_id, "vl") ~ "vl_page",
      str_detect(section_id, "failure") ~ "vl_page",
      str_detect(section_id, "ontime") ~ "inj_page",
      str_detect(section_id, "_pt") ~ "inj_page",
      str_detect(section_id, "1$") ~ "demographics_page",
      str_detect(section_id, "1b$") ~ "demo_by_lai",
      str_detect(section_id, "2$|assessed|2_event") ~ "Assessed",
      str_detect(section_id, "3$|counseled|3_event") ~ "Counseled",
      str_detect(section_id, "4$|interested|4_event") ~ "Interested",
      str_detect(section_id, "5$|screened|5_event") ~ "Screened",
      str_detect(section_id, "6$|eligible|6_event") ~ "Eligible",
      str_detect(section_id, "7$|prescribed") ~ "Prescribed",
      str_detect(section_id, "7a$|accessible") ~ "Accessible",
      str_detect(section_id, "8$|initiated") ~ "Initiated",
      str_detect(section_id, "9$|sustained|discontinued") ~ "Sustained",
    ),
    group_name = case_when(
      str_detect(section_id, "sex") ~ "Sex",
      str_detect(section_id, "race") ~ "Race",
      str_detect(section_id, "ethnicity") ~ "Ethnicity",
      str_detect(section_id, "age") ~ "Age",
      str_detect(section_id, "insurance") ~ "HIV Medication Payor",
